{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyWithoutMFA",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "NotAction": [
        "iam:CreateVirtualMFADevice",
        "iam:EnableMFADevice",
        "iam:GetUser",
        "iam:ListMFADevices",
        "iam:ListVirtualMFADevices",
        "iam:ResyncMFADevice",
        "sts:GetSessionToken"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "BoolIfExists": {
          "aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "false"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}

/*
  HOW THIS POLICY WORKS:

  Effect: Deny
  Denies all AWS actions EXCEPT those listed in NotAction.

  NotAction (exceptions from the Deny):
  - iam:CreateVirtualMFADevice  — create the MFA device record
  - iam:EnableMFADevice         — link the device to the user account
  - iam:GetUser                 — view own user details
  - iam:ListMFADevices          — check what MFA devices are registered
  - iam:ListVirtualMFADevices   — list available virtual MFA devices
  - iam:ResyncMFADevice         — resync if codes are out of sync
  - sts:GetSessionToken         — obtain a session token after MFA enrolment

  Without these exceptions in NotAction, a user locked out by this policy
  could not even set up MFA — creating a permanent lockout requiring admin
  intervention for every new user. The exceptions are required.

  Condition: BoolIfExists aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent = false
  The Deny only fires when the session was authenticated WITHOUT MFA.
  If MFA was used at login, aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent = true and the
  condition does not match — the Deny does not trigger.

  BoolIfExists (not Bool):
  BoolIfExists handles the case where the key is absent (CLI calls
  using access keys never have this key set). Bool would fail in those
  cases. BoolIfExists evaluates: "if the key exists and is false, deny".

  ATTACH TO:
  All user groups except the IAM Administrators group.
  The Administrators group should enforce MFA separately at the role
  assumption level to avoid edge cases.

  RESULT:
  A user who logs in without MFA can ONLY set up MFA. Once they
  re-authenticate with MFA, normal permissions are restored.
  A user who tries to skip MFA setup is locked out indefinitely until
  they enrol.
*/
